# aether_muqabala — broadband-aether-mediated cybernetic-feedback patch.
# Tests whether the dimensionless-aether substrate from aether_sympathy
# .aither generalises from sympathetic-resonance (open-loop coupling
# between resonators) to full cybernetic dynamics (the al-Muqabala
# tradition's chaotic-but-regulated temporal motion).
#
# VERDICT — PARTIAL POSITIVE. The aether substrate enables CYBERNETIC
# STABILISATION (the loop finds an attractor and locks the system into
# it, with audible spectral breathing on top), and the falsification
# check confirms the loop is doing real work. The patch has a real
# regime structure across parameter space — five qualitatively distinct
# sounds reachable by knob movement — where the original al_muqabala
# .aither produced one fixed "fire-alarm" sound regardless of knobs.
#
# What it does NOT achieve: the rich attractor-jumping al-Mukabala
# motion the analog tradition produces (the "system finds a new
# attractor and lingers there, then breaks out" behaviour). In every
# regime the patch produces, EITHER the spectrum is locked OR the
# spectrum is noise-driven; the patch lacks the multi-stable hysteresis
# the analog wave-multipliers provide. Diagnosis at end of header.
#
# CONTRAST WITH al_muqabala.aither
#   The earlier al_muqabala patch hand-wired feedback through ONE state
#   cell ($loop). It produced a fixed-pitch sweeping tone — "fire alarm,
#   very consistent" at default knobs — not the temporally evolving
#   motion the analog tradition is about. With no aether, the closed
#   loop carried only one signal: the carrier's own state, ring-modded
#   and folded. There was no broadband medium for the loop to live in.
#
#   This patch operates on the dimensionless broadband aether instead.
#   The structural changes:
#     - The driver writes broadband disturbance (continuous noise +
#       LFO-modulated amplitude) into $aether.
#     - The carrier READS $aether's energy envelope as FM, AND reads
#       the raw $aether as additive bias into the wavefolder.
#     - The carrier's nonlinear output is WRITTEN BACK into $aether,
#       providing the carrier-harmonic broadband return path.
#     - The observer ($obs) is the envelope follower on $aether.
#     - The regulator pulls back the noise floor and biases the fold
#       amount when $obs gets high — the al-Muqabala "negative-feedback
#       regulator" applied to the energy injection AND to the
#       nonlinearity itself.
#
# AUDIT — DEFAULT KNOBS (carrierToAether = 0.8, regStrength = 0.4)
#   Single 30 s FFT (`./aither audit patches/aether_muqabala.aither 30`):
#     RMS  ≈ -22 dB    Peak ≈ -16 dB    Dominant ≈ 254 Hz
#
#   Windowed 30 × 1 s (tests/temporal_audit.nim):
#     RMS         μ = -21.65 dB    σ = 0.79 dB    ← audible breathing
#     centroid    μ = 15457 Hz     σ = 1251 Hz    ← spectral motion
#     fundamental μ = 114 Hz       σ = 625 Hz     ← jumpy autocorr
#     dominant Pk μ = 254 Hz       σ = 0.2 Hz     ← LOCKED ATTRACTOR
#
#   Interpretation: the loop has STABILISED the carrier at 254 Hz and
#   it stays there for the full 30 s. The LFO produces ±1 dB amplitude
#   breathing and ±1 kHz centroid breathing on top. The dominant peak
#   never moves — peak σ = 0.2 Hz across 30 windows is essentially zero.
#
# FALSIFICATION CHECK — set `carrierToAether = 0.0` and re-audit
#   With the cybernetic loop broken (carrier still reads aether but
#   does NOT write back), the same 30 × 1 s windows produce:
#     RMS         μ = -19.45 dB   σ = 0.02 dB    ← no breathing
#     centroid    μ =  6867 Hz    σ = 396 Hz     ← LFO-driven only
#     fundamental μ = 0 Hz        σ = 0 Hz       ← no detected periodicity
#     dominant Pk μ = 366 Hz      σ = 260 Hz     ← WANDERS with LFO
#
#   The differences are unambiguous:
#     - With loop ON: peak LOCKED (σ=0.2), spectral motion 3× higher,
#       fundamental detectable (114 Hz)
#     - With loop OFF: peak WANDERS (σ=260), no fundamental, lower
#       spectral motion, no amplitude breathing
#   The cybernetic loop is doing real work. It STABILISES the system
#   into one attractor; without it the patch is open-loop noise + LFO.
#   This is NOT variant-C; the loop's contribution is large and clean.
#
# REGIME STRUCTURE — what the parameter space looks like
#   (per tests/sweep_muqabala.nim — 8 s renders, 1 s windows)
#
#   carrierToAether sweep (loop gain), default reg = 0.4:
#     0.0   peak σ = 348 Hz   ← open loop, LFO wandering
#     0.2   peak σ =  25 Hz   ← partial lock
#     0.5   peak σ =  35 Hz   ← marginal, RMS σ = 1.0 dB (most breathing)
#     0.8   peak σ = 0.4 Hz   ← LOCKED at 254 Hz   (default)
#     1.5   peak σ = 221 Hz   ← LOCKED at 22 kHz   (high-freq attractor)
#     3.0   peak σ = 7625 Hz  ← saturated noise regime
#    10.0   peak σ = 7155 Hz  ← saturated noise regime
#
#   regStrength sweep (regulator pull), default cToA = 0.8:
#     0.0   peak σ = 8424 Hz  ← saturated noise regime
#     0.1   peak σ = 9313 Hz  ← saturated noise regime
#     0.4   peak σ =  0.4 Hz  ← LOCKED at 254 Hz   (default)
#     0.8   peak σ =  0.4 Hz  ← LOCKED at 254 Hz
#     1.5   peak σ =  0.7 Hz  ← LOCKED at 156 Hz   (sub-tonal attractor)
#     3.0   peak σ =  0.7 Hz  ← LOCKED at 156 Hz
#
#   The patch has FIVE qualitatively distinct regimes:
#     A. Open-loop LFO wandering         (cToA = 0)
#     B. Marginal breathing              (cToA ≈ 0.5, reg ≈ 0.4)
#     C. Locked at 254 Hz                (cToA = 0.8, reg = 0.4)   ← default
#     D. Locked at 156 Hz (sub-tonal)    (cToA = 0.8, reg ≥ 1.5)
#     E. Locked at 22 kHz (high-freq)    (cToA ≈ 1.5)
#     F. Saturated noise regime          (cToA ≥ 3 OR reg ≤ 0.1)
#
#   Important caveat about regime F: the "wandering" peak there
#   (peak σ ≈ 8 kHz) is mostly NOISE-DRIVEN, not loop-driven. With
#   carrierToAether = 0 in low-regulator config, peak σ is still
#   ≈ 8000 Hz — the loop adds little. The loop's real contribution
#   shows in regimes B–E (where falsification matters).
#
# WHAT THE PATCH SOUNDS LIKE (as the analyser describes — user
# verification pending live evaluation):
#   At default knobs: a low droning hum at 254 Hz with broadband
#   noise content swelling and ebbing on a 14 s cycle. Harmonic
#   content shifts as the LFO sweeps, but the fundamental never
#   moves. Closer to "drone with breathing harmonics" than to
#   "rich evolving motion."
#
# DIAGNOSIS — why "rich al-Mukabala motion" doesn't emerge
#   The al-Mukabala tradition produces "events": moments where the
#   system finds a new attractor and lingers, then breaks out. That
#   requires MULTI-STABLE memory in the loop — the system must be
#   able to settle in different operating points and need a kick to
#   leave each one. The analog wave-multipliers provide this through
#   their nonlinear transfer function's smooth bumps and analog state
#   (capacitor charge, slow integration of the bias network).
#
#   This aither patch's nonlinearities are:
#     - fold(x, amount): zigzag — STATELESS
#     - drive(x, amount): smooth saturator x/(1+|x|) — STATELESS
#   The only stateful elements are $aether (a leaky integrator) and
#   $obs (an envelope follower on $aether). Both have simple,
#   predictable dynamics. The loop's only memory is "current aether
#   value" — a single number — which can settle to one equilibrium
#   but can't represent multiple competing attractors.
#
#   What would unlock al-Mukabala dynamics, in approximate order of
#   structural cost:
#     1. Add a slow comparator-triggered "kick" to the regulator
#        when $obs crosses a threshold (the cybernetics.md episode-8
#        extension). Discrete events that pop the system out.
#     2. Add a stateful nonlinearity in the loop — e.g., a
#        compander with attack/release, or a hysteresis comparator
#        like a Schmitt trigger. State in the loop's gain, not just
#        in its medium.
#     3. Replace the carrier oscillator with a relaxation oscillator
#        whose period is set by an integrator — the "wave-multiplier"
#        analogue of the analog tradition.
#     4. Stack multiple aether cells with cross-coupling. Each can
#        be in a different attractor; their coupling drives jumps.
#     5. Add a delay line in the loop. Time-delay feedback in
#        nonlinear systems is a textbook chaos-generator.
#   Whichever direction is tried, the right move is structural — the
#   patch needs more state than the current architecture provides.
#
# WHAT THIS RESULT VINDICATES vs. the broadband-aether sympathy result
#   The aether enables ONE form of cybernetic behaviour (attractor
#   stabilisation via closed-loop coupling) but NOT another (rich
#   chaotic temporal motion). This is a more nuanced result than the
#   sympathy positive: the aether is a cybernetic *enabler*, not a
#   cybernetic *one-stop-shop*. The medium is necessary; it isn't
#   sufficient. See pole.md "Aether enables cybernetic stabilisation
#   but not full al-Mukabala dynamics (2026-04-29)" for the full write-
#   up and what experiments make sense next.
#
# AUDIT METHODOLOGY
#   - `./aither audit patches/aether_muqabala.aither 30` for a 30 s
#     single-FFT (smears across regimes; flat-spectrum if not).
#   - `nim r --hints:off --warnings:off --threads:on tests/temporal_audit
#     .nim patches/aether_muqabala.aither 30.0 1.0` for windowed
#     analysis: per-second RMS, centroid, fundamental, dominant peak.
#     Aggregate stddev across 30 windows is the cybernetic diagnostic.
#   - `nim r --hints:off --warnings:off --threads:on tests/sweep_muqabala
#     .nim` for full parameter-sweep regime characterisation.

$aether = 0.0
$obs    = 0.0

# --- driver / strike parameters -------------------------------------
let floorAmp        = 4.0       # base amplitude of continuous noise floor
let aetherWrite     = 1.0       # gain on noise floor → aether
let aetherDecay     = 0.995     # per-sample aether leak (slower than sympathy)
let driverDepth     = 0.7       # LFO modulation depth on noise floor (0..1)
let driverRateHz    = 0.07      # ~14 s driver cycle
let regStrength     = 0.4       # regulator pull strength. With this value
                                # the loop's role is STABILISATION — it
                                # locks the system into one attractor.
                                # Lower values (≤ 0.1) give a "wandering"
                                # spectrum but the wandering is mostly
                                # noise-driven, not loop-driven (the loop
                                # fails the falsification check there).

# --- carrier parameters ---------------------------------------------
let carrierFreq     = 80.0      # carrier base frequency (Hz)
let carrierAmp      = 0.5       # raw carrier amplitude
let carrierCoupling = 0.3       # gain on raw $aether → carrier (additive)
let fmDepthHz       = 60.0      # gain on $obs → carrier FREQUENCY (Hz).
                                # This is the wandering knob — the carrier's
                                # pitch shifts up as $obs rises.
let foldAmount      = 1.4       # base wavefolder drive
let foldModDepth    = 1.5       # gain on $obs → wavefolder amount
                                # (state-dependent loop nonlinearity)
let driveAmount     = 2.5       # saturator drive
let carrierToAether = 0.8       # Loop-gain knob. At this value with the
                                # default regulator, the loop STABILISES
                                # the system into one attractor at 254 Hz.
                                # Higher values (≥ 1.5) push the system
                                # into a "wandering peak" regime, but in
                                # that regime the wandering is mostly
                                # noise-driven, not loop-driven (see header
                                # parameter-sweep table for details).
                                # FALSIFICATION: set to 0.0 to break the
                                # carrier-to-aether feedback loop. The
                                # carrier still reads the aether (additive
                                # bias and FM), so it still makes sound;
                                # but the cybernetic loop is broken.

play coupled:
  # --- driver: slow LFO modulates a continuous broadband floor ---
  let lfo        = (sin(TAU * phasor(driverRateHz)) + 1) * 0.5
  let lfoEnv     = 1 - driverDepth + driverDepth * lfo
  let noiseFloor = noise() * floorAmp * lfoEnv

  # --- observer: envelope follower on the aether's amplitude ---
  $obs = lp1(abs($aether), 5.0)
  let pullback = max(0.1, 1 - $obs * regStrength)

  # --- driver writes broadband floor (regulated) into the aether ---
  $aether = $aether * aetherDecay + noiseFloor * aetherWrite * pullback

  # --- carrier with state-dependent FM and state-dependent fold amount.
  # --- $obs (slow aether energy) modulates BOTH the carrier's frequency
  # --- AND the wavefolder's bias. This is the al-Muqabala move: the
  # --- system's energy reshapes the system's nonlinearity.
  let modFreq  = max(20.0, carrierFreq + $obs * fmDepthHz)
  let dynFold  = foldAmount + $obs * foldModDepth
  let carrier  = sin(TAU * phasor(modFreq)) * carrierAmp
  let carrierFb = carrier + $aether * carrierCoupling
  let folded   = fold(carrierFb, dynFold)
  let driven   = drive(folded, driveAmount)

  # --- KEY CYBERNETIC MOVE: carrier writes broadband output back ---
  $aether = $aether + driven * carrierToAether

  let s = driven * 0.2
  [s, s]

coupled
